+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+- **Zero Density Regions**: Standard free-flight sampling places samples proportional to the product of transmittance and density (extinction-weighted). In empty or low-density regions, this leads to high variance for gradients because samples are rarely placed there, yet gradients may have non-trivial magnitude (e.g., to reconstruct an object where there was previously just air) [S1]. DRT mitigates this by sampling proportional to just transmittance [S1].
+- **Local Minima**: Optimization of scattering parameters can get stuck in local minima. This can be alleviated by bootstrapping the optimization from nonphysical emissive volumes (similar to voxel-based radiance fields) [S1].
+- **Majorant Selection**: The majorant $\bar{\sigma}$ must be greater than or equal to the maximum extinction coefficient $\sigma_t(t)$ along the ray to ensure valid sampling from the homogenized medium [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** With `use_drt_subsampling` disabled, the runtime is $O(n^2)$ with respect to path length $n$ due to per-vertex gradient estimation [S2, unofficial]. With subsampling enabled, it reduces to $O(n)$ [S2, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage remains reasonable even for high-resolution volumes due to efficient data structures in the underlying renderer [S1].
-- **Performance:** DRT serves as a baseline [S1]. In multi-view scenarios, it is significantly slower than single-scattering methods because it must recompute gradients for each view, whereas single-scattering methods pre-compute view-independent terms [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Unbiased estimation of in-scattering gradients; robust in low-density regions where free-flight sampling fails [S2, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Computationally expensive for high sample counts; no pre-computation advantage for multi-view rendering [S1].
-- **When to use:** When unbiased gradients are required for general volume paths, especially in low-density regimes, and multi-view speedups are not the primary concern.
-- **When not to use:** When single scattering is sufficient and multi-view performance is critical; use single-scattering differentiable methods instead [S1].
+- **Time Complexity**: Runs in linear time with respect to the number of steps [S1]. When integrated with path replay backpropagation, it uses constant memory [S1]. However, if `use_drt_subsampling` is false, the runtime can be $O(n^2)$ where $n$ is the number of path vertices, because DRT is applied at every vertex [S3, unofficial]. With subsampling enabled, it reduces to $O(n)$ [S3, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity**: Constant memory when integrated with path replay backpropagation, as it uses a reservoir to store only one segment [S1].
+- **Performance**: DRT's Monte Carlo estimation becomes more expensive with higher sample counts (SPP), leading to increasing speedup advantages for pre-computed methods like [Single-Scattering Differentiable Volume Rendering](4143-single-scattering-differentiable-volume-rendering.md) at higher SPP [S2]. For example, at 512 SPP, the single-scattering method shows a 6.3x speedup over DRT [S2].
+- **Strengths**: Unbiased, yields low-variance gradients, and handles empty/low-density regions better than standard free-flight sampling [S1].
+- **Limitations**: Must recompute gradients for each view, unlike pre-computed methods [S2]. More expensive than single-scattering methods at high sample counts [S2].
+- **When to Use**: When using physically-based scattering and absorbing volumes and when gradient variance in low-density regions is a concern [S1]. When using [Single-Scattering Differentiable Volume Rendering](4143-single-scattering-differentiable-volume-rendering.md) is not feasible due to multiple scattering or complex geometry.
+- **When Not to Use**: When high sample counts are required and pre-computed methods are applicable, as DRT's cost scales with SPP [S2]. When using NeRF-style emissive volumes, as DRT is designed for scattering/absorbing volumes [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Differential Ratio Tracing was implemented in Mitsuba 3 as a differentiable rendering method [S1]. It was introduced as a baseline for comparison in research on unbiased inverse volume rendering, specifically to address the limitations of standard free-flight path replay backpropagation [S2, unofficial]. The method is part of the broader effort to enable stable gradient-based optimization for volume reconstruction tasks [S2, unofficial].
+Differential Ratio Tracing was introduced in the paper "Unbiased inverse volume rendering with differential trackers" [S1]. It was implemented as a baseline in Mitsuba 3 [S2]. The method addresses the limitations of standard differentiable volume rendering techniques that use free-flight sampling, which can produce biased and high-variance gradients [S1]. It is part of the family of differentiable rendering methods that use automatic differentiation in reverse/adjoint mode [Parent].
 
